= 1. The Challenge of Establishing Democracy in Free India

When India achieved independence, it undertook an unprecedented democratic experiment. While contemporary political theorists believed that democracy could survive only in wealthy, literate, industrialized societies, Indian leaders staked their future on immediate *universal adult franchise*:

== The Constitutional Mandate & Setting Up the Machinery
- *The Election Commission of India:* Formally constituted in *January 1950* under Article 324 of the Constitution to superintend, direct, and control the entire electoral process.
- *The First Chief Election Commissioner:* *Sukumar Sen*, an exceptional Indian Civil Service (ICS) administrator, was appointed as the first CEC, tasked with building electoral machinery from scratch across a subcontinent.
- *The Unprecedented Scale of the First General Election:*
  - *17.32 crore eligible voters*, over *85% of whom were completely illiterate*.
  - Over 3,200 Members of Legislative Assemblies (MLAs) and 489 Members of Parliament (Lok Sabha) were to be elected simultaneously across 2.24 lakh polling stations.
  - *The Identity Crisis:* In early rolls, nearly 40 lakh female voters had been enrolled merely as "wife of..." or "daughter of...". Sukumar Sen courageously rejected these rolls, ordering a fresh survey to ensure every Indian woman was registered by her own independent name.

== The Skepticism of Western and Domestic Critics
Western observers and domestic conservatives ridiculed the idea of universal adult franchise in an impoverished, caste-divided society:
- A prominent Indian magazine editor called it *"the biggest gamble in history"*.
- An ex-ICS British civil servant wrote in *The Organiser* that Nehru *"would live to confess the failure of universal adult franchise in India"*.
- The first general election was conducted between *October 1951 and February 1952*, lasting nearly six months.
- *The Resounding Triumph:* Polling took place peacefully with high voter turnout, defying all skeptics and proving that democracy could take deep, organic roots in a non-Western, developing society.

